WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.991
Apple just released the 2nd developer beta
of iPadOS, and with it comes the new SMB Server

00:00:04.991 --> 00:00:06.140
connection feature.

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:10.410
With iPadOS, we’re already able to connect
everything from hard drives to flash drives

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.690
to easily access files and copy them to the
local storage.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:16.950
We have a full guide on how to do that, which
you can check out by clicking the card above,

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:20.770
but for this video, we’re gonna show you
how to wirelessly connect to a hard drive,

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:23.370
NAS or server over an SMB connection.

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:27.340
The beauty of this is that if you love using
your iPad for editing photos or videos or

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.961
doing production work, you’ll be able to
access all of the files on your hard drive

00:00:30.961 --> 00:00:34.720
or server as long as you’re connected to
the same WiFi connection that your router

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:35.720
outputs.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:39.890
Modern WiFi routers allow you to connect storage
to them, either by a regular USB connection

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:41.700
or with an ethernet port.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.960
The more advanced server storage systems include
ethernet ports for simple connection straight

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:50.760
into a router, and they also have applications
and instructions on how to connect using SMB.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.570
The common person won’t have one of these,
they’ll have a regular hard drive, SSD or

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:58.110
maybe even a flash drive they want to connect
to, so let’s go through the steps on how

00:00:58.110 --> 00:00:59.110
to do this.

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:02.780
First off, you’ll need to access your router's
settings through a web browser that’s connected

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:04.650
to your router’s WiFi network.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:08.450
If you’re not sure how to do that, refer
to your router’s user manual.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:10.759
Our router tells us to go to this website.

00:01:10.759 --> 00:01:14.179
We already set it all up and created a password,
so we’ll just log in.

00:01:14.179 --> 00:01:18.590
Now here in our dashboard, we see a section
for external storage which is empty, so let’s

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:22.020
go ahead and plug in a simple flash drive
into the USB port on the back.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:27.520
Back at the router settings page, our drive
now shows up, so let’s click manage storage.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.130
And right here, you can see a Mac access address
with SMB right in the beginning of it.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:36.640
Now let’s go back to our iPad, go to the
Files app and tap this little edit icon within

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.109
the Browse tab.

00:01:38.109 --> 00:01:42.399
Then tap connect to server, and type in that
SMB address and hit connect.

00:01:42.399 --> 00:01:46.429
Now you can simply connect as a guest, but
if that doesn’t work, you can register as

00:01:46.429 --> 00:01:47.429
a user.

00:01:47.429 --> 00:01:51.539
Most user accounts will be named Admin by
default, and the password should be the same

00:01:51.539 --> 00:01:53.990
password you use to log into router settings.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:57.609
If not, you can read through your router’s
manual to set up your external storage and

00:01:57.609 --> 00:01:59.079
account name and password.

00:01:59.079 --> 00:02:02.789
We already have a large 100TB NAS with an
SMB server setup, and we’ve logged into

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:03.929
it on our iPad.

00:02:03.929 --> 00:02:08.469
So here it is within the Files app, and as
you can see, we now have access to up to 100TB

00:02:08.469 --> 00:02:12.650
of the files we use every day for editing
videos and everything else we do.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:16.409
Browsing through all of the files is super
quick, no lag or load times at all.

00:02:16.409 --> 00:02:17.409
Unfortunately, thumbnails aren’t currently
showing up, but if you tap into something

00:02:17.409 --> 00:02:18.409
like an image, it shows up perfectly fine.

00:02:18.409 --> 00:02:21.069
And here’s a video playing, it opens up
just fine and plays back fairly smooth but

00:02:21.069 --> 00:02:24.740
not perfect since it’s over the internet
and not straight off a physically connected

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:25.740
drive.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:30.739
We can easily tap and hold a file and tap
on multiple files, then open the On My iPad

00:02:30.739 --> 00:02:32.849
location, and drop it right in.

00:02:32.849 --> 00:02:36.939
Surprisingly, the transfer speed is very quick
considering it’s downloading the files using

00:02:36.939 --> 00:02:41.200
WiFi, and there you have it, those files are
now on local storage.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:45.099
Even better, let’s use split-view to open
up the LumaFusion video editor.

00:02:45.099 --> 00:02:50.109
Now, we can drag and drop files straight from
the SMB server into LumaFusion, and start

00:02:50.109 --> 00:02:51.109
editing.

00:02:51.109 --> 00:02:54.900
This is great because if you make any changes
to files on your server on your Mac or computer,

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.590
it’ll instantly be updated on the iPad.

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:01.590
If we didn’t have SMB server support, we’d
have to transfer files from the server to

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:04.170
a flash drive, then from the flash drive to
the iPad.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.773
This just makes it so much more convenient
to work on your iPad.

00:03:05.773 --> 00:03:08.520
If you haven’t watched our iPadOS multitasking
guide, click you can check it out right there

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.459
and if you enjoyed this video, tap like and
hit that circle above to subscribe so you

00:03:12.459 --> 00:03:14.060
don’t miss out on more videos like this
one.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:19.310
Thanks for watching, and we’ll see you in
the next one!

